A||||3||BENGAL4||OUTSIDE DOOR HANDLE
Z||000000||BENGAL4||07-21-99||01:38 PM||||Hey Guys, My paasenger side door handle broke and I was wondering how hard is it to replace and where can you get a replacement. I looked for one in the FieroStore catalog but did not see one. Any suggestions? <br>
Z||000001||batboy||07-21-99||06:40 PM||||The outer door handle? You have to take off the inner door panel in order to get at the 2 nuts that hold the door handle on. I believe the outer door handle is a standard GM item for that era cars, if so, then you can pick it up at about any auto parts store in the "help" section in those red packages.<p>If it's the inner door handle, you still have to remove the inner door panel. The inner handle is probably going to be a salvage yard item, unless the dealer can still order it.<p>The door panel is removed by taking off the window crank (unless it was power windows), the arm rest, and that black plastic inner door handle trim plate. There are two hidden screws (one is behind the little black Fiero emblem next to the inner door handle and the other is on the arm rest). You have to carefully pop these little square covers off with a small screwdriver. Disconnect the locking rod from the sliding lock knob and carefully pull the pop fasteners free from around the edges of the door panel. This job is not too tough, but don't get in a hurry because it's easy to break something if you try using brute force.
Z||000002||BENGAL4||07-21-99||07:07 PM||||Got the door panel off and carefully peeled back the paper and there it was. if my hands were smaller, it would be easier. I will go to the parts store and see if they have one in the red "help" section. By the way, has anyone ever changed a door lock? I have a key broken off in the lock on the same side door. Since I'm in there, I might as well tackle that as well. Any suggestions?<p><br>Bill
Z||000003||batboy||07-22-99||05:50 AM||||If you already have the door panel off, you've got the hard part of changing the lock cylinder done. Disconnect the locking rod from the cylinder, remove the flat U shaped spring clip and the lock cylinder is off. Try to get the broken key out by using small sharp objects to carefully pry it out or take it to a locksmith. This will allow you to keep using the same key for both doors (I'm hoping you had a spare).
